        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes With Leaf Vacuums
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Vacuuming Wet Leaves</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Damp leaves pack the tube, coat the impeller and turn the bag into a heavy sack of paste. It is the leading cause of clogs and lost suction. Wait for a dry afternoon, spread wet mats out to dry first, or let a mulching mower handle a soggy layer on the lawn instead of the vacuum.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on Blower Airspeed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 250 MPH rating describes blower mode. It says nothing about how finely the machine shreds or how much the bag holds, and those decide how often you stop. Compare mulch ratio, bag capacity and impeller material first, and treat airspeed as a tiebreaker for the blowing half of the job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Thin Extension Cord</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A light indoor cord over a long run drops voltage, heats up and starves a 12 to 14 amp motor, which shows up as weak suction and a warm handle. Use an outdoor-rated cord of the gauge your manual specifies, uncoil it fully, and plug into a GFCI-protected outlet every time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Clearing a Jam With Power Connected</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Reaching into the intake while the cord is plugged in or the battery is fitted is how fingers meet impellers. Unplug or pull the battery, wait for the impeller to stop completely, then clear the jam with gloves or a stick. Treat changing modes and emptying the bag the same way.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Vacuuming an Entire Open Lawn</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A handheld intake is a few inches wide and the bag holds about a bushel, so vacuuming a whole lawn takes hours. Blow or sweep leaves into piles first, then vacuum the piles and the beds. On big open lawns, a mulching mower or a push sweeper does the bulk work far faster.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

