        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best leaf vacuum for most people?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The WORX WG509 TriVac suits most suburban yards. WORX rates it at an 18:1 mulch ratio, the highest of the handhelds we picked, with a two-stage metal impeller, a 1.2-bushel bag, 9.3 pounds of weight and a 3-year warranty. If you want a bigger bag, the Sun Joe SBJ606E holds 18 gallons; if you want more vacuum airflow, the Toro UltraPlus lists 405 CFM.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a leaf vacuum better than a leaf blower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They do different jobs. A blower moves leaves into piles quickly across open ground; a vacuum picks up and shreds those piles and cleans beds, corners and window wells a blower only scatters. Most 3-in-1 vacuums blow too, so one tool covers a small yard. For big open lawns, a dedicated blower plus a mulching mower usually works better than bagging everything.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What mulch ratio should I look for in a leaf vacuum?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Look for 16:1 or higher on a corded model; that means sixteen bags of loose, dry leaves shrink to one. The WORX WG509 is rated 18:1, while compact cordless models sit lower, around 12:1 on the WORX WG583. Treat ratios as relative, because makers rate them with dry leaves, and owners report less reduction when leaves are damp or mixed with twigs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a leaf vacuum pick up pine needles and acorns?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Pine needles go through most leaf vacuums, though they mulch less than broad leaves and can bridge across the intake in thick mats. Acorns, pine cones, sticks and stones are a different story: they jam the tube and can chip or bend the impeller. Rake or pick out hard debris first, and a metal impeller survives the occasional miss far better than plastic.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are cordless leaf vacuums powerful enough?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For beds, patios and small yards, yes. The WORX WG583 lists 350 CFM on high and the Ryobi 40V mulches at 16:1. Runtime is the limit: Ryobi quotes over 30 minutes on a 4Ah battery, which will not clear a large wooded lot in one go. Owners with big yards keep a second battery charging or use a corded model for the heavy fall cleanup.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a leaf vacuum to clean gutters?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some models support it. Sun Joe sells the SBJ606E as a 4-in-1 with a gutter-cleaning attachment, which lets you blow debris out of gutters from the ground. A standard handheld vac does not reach a roof line safely. If gutter cleaning is a recurring chore, our guide to the <a href="/best-gutter-guards/">best gutter guards</a> covers ways to keep leaves out of gutters in the first place.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I stop my leaf vacuum from clogging?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Vacuum only dry leaves, feed the tube into the edge of a pile rather than its center, and keep sticks, cones and stones out. Empty the bag before it is packed full, and shake out fine dust after each session so the bag can breathe. If it jams, unplug it or remove the battery and wait for the impeller to stop before clearing the intake.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
